WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 4: Thirteen Minutes: The Lone Clockmaker Who Almost Killed Hitler

1
00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:10.720
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories, where we uncover the real, well sourced, and lesser known tales behind history's most prominent figures.

2
00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:12.560
[Rebecca] I'm your host, Rebecca.

3
00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:17.600
[Rebecca] History often remembers the great tyrants and the vast armies that clashed to bring them down.

4
00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:28.800
[Rebecca] Yet, sometimes, the fate of 50 million human lives hinges not upon a battlefield, but upon the solitary conscience of a quiet craftsman working by flashlight in the dead of night.

5
00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:34.160
[Rebecca] In November of 1939, Adolf Hitler stood at the pinnacle of his demonic power.

6
00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:42.400
[Rebecca] Poland had been crushed in four weeks, the Western democracies were paralyzed, and the German military machine seemed unstoppable.

7
00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:50.800
[Rebecca] That autumn, Hitler stepped onto the podium at the Munich Bujebrayukler to deliver his annual triumphal address to the Nazi faithful.

8
00:00:50.800 --> 00:01:00.960
[Rebecca] He had no idea that directly behind his back, concealed inside a massive stone support pillar, a homemade mechanical bomb was ticking toward detonation.

9
00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:10.560
[Rebecca] What happened over the next 80 minutes and the 13 minutes that decided the destiny of the world is one of the greatest true historical thrillers ever recorded.

10
00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:13.840
[George] Here is George with the story.

11
00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:31.520
[George] Slender, soft-spoken, and deliberate, Elser was a master woodworker and precision cabinet maker.

12
00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:42.240
[George] He was a man of few words who preferred the company of his lathe, his hand chisels, and his zither, which he played with delicate musical skill in the local tavern on Sunday evenings.

13
00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.960
[George] Elser belonged to no political party.

14
00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:50.320
[George] He had never made a political speech in his life, and he loathed public demonstrations.

15
00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:57.280
[George] Yet, Elser possessed something increasingly rare in 1930s Germany absolute moral clarity.

16
00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:05.880
[George] While millions of his fellow countrymen succumbed to the hypnotic hysteria of the Nazi regime, Elser watched with cold, growing horror.

17
00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:16.520
[George] Working in an armaments factory in Heidenheim, he observed how consumer production was being secretly replaced by artillery shells, tank armor, and machine gun mounts.

18
00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:26.200
[George] He saw how the trade unions were abolished, how working class wages were ruthlessly slashed, and how religious liberties were being systematically dismantled.

19
00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:39.960
[George] In the autumn of 1938, when British Prime Minister Neville Chamberlain returned from Munich waving his piece of paper promising peace in our time, the world breathed a collective sigh of relief, but Jorg Elser knew better.

20
00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:48.200
[George] Elser concluded with absolute certainty that Hitler was marching Europe directly into a catastrophic world war of unimaginable slaughter.

21
00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:51.560
[George] And he came to a staggering, solitary decision.

22
00:02:51.560 --> 00:03:03.000
[George] Because the generals and politicians lacked the courage to act, he would take it upon himself to eliminate Adolf Hitler, Hermann Göring, and Joseph Goebbels in a single, devastating blow.

23
00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:07.720
[George] Elser knew that striking at Hitler was an almost impossible military challenge.

24
00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:16.360
[George] The Fura was surrounded day and night by rings of elite SS bodyguards, motorized convoys, and obsessive security precautions.

25
00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:21.960
[George] Yet, Elser identified a singular, glaring vulnerability in Hitler's schedule.

26
00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:34.520
[George] Every year on the evening of November 8, Hitler returned like clockwork to the Bujebrayukler, a sprawling, historic beer hall in Munich, where the infamous Beer Hall Putsch of 1923 had been launched.

27
00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:45.760
[George] There, surrounded by his old party comrades, Hitler always delivered a lengthy, impassioned address lasting between two and three hours, broadcast live over national radio.

28
00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:51.040
[George] In November of 1938, Elser traveled to Munich to observe the rally in person.

29
00:03:51.280 --> 00:04:00.880
[George] Mingling quietly with the crowd, Elser studied the Grand Hall not as a political observer, but with the trained, calculating eye of an expert builder.

30
00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:06.000
[George] He noticed a massive, square stone pillar directly behind the speaker's podium.

31
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:14.080
[George] That single stone pillar supported the heavy timber balcony above, as well as the main structural roof trusses of the entire hall.

32
00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:20.720
[George] If that pillar could be severed, the entire roof would collapse directly onto the speaker's platform like an avalanche.

33
00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:32.800
[George] Elser took out a tailor's measuring tape, calculated the dimensions of the pillar, noted the layout of the doors and windows, and quietly boarded the train back home to Kernigsbronn to begin preparations.

34
00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:38.480
[George] To bring down a reinforced stone pillar, Elser needed industrial grade explosives.

35
00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:44.560
[George] Because he was a civilian with no criminal connections, he could not purchase dynamite on the black market.

36
00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:49.360
[George] Instead, he found employment at the Wallmer Quarry in nearby Königsbronn.

37
00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:53.600
[George] For months, Elser worked grueling shifts in the limestone dust.

38
00:04:53.600 --> 00:05:04.000
[George] Day after day, with icy nerves, he smuggled out sticks of Donarit explosive and blasting caps, hiding them one by one in his lunch pail and his deep coat pockets.

39
00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:17.520
[George] By the spring of 1939, Elser had stockpiled 105 sticks of high-grade commercial explosive, amounting to roughly 100 pounds of dynamite, along with hundreds of precision detonator caps.

40
00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:32.440
[George] In August of 1939, as German panzer divisions mobilized along the Polish frontier, Elser packed two heavy wooden suitcases with his explosives, his woodworking tools and his clothes, and moved into a rented room in Munich.

41
00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:38.600
[George] He informed his landlady that he was an independent inventor working on a secret mechanical patent.

42
00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:44.040
[George] In reality, Georg Elser was preparing to launch a one-man war against the Third Reich.

43
00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:51.400
[George] Beginning on the night of August 28, 1939, Georg Elser began an ordeal of superhuman endurance.

44
00:05:51.400 --> 00:06:00.280
[George] Every evening at nine o'clock, Elser entered the Bujabrayukular, ordered a modest dinner of sausage and beer, and sat quietly in the corner.

45
00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:12.520
[George] Just before closing time at 10:30, when the waiters were distracted cleaning tables, Elser slipped unnoticed up a wooden staircase and hid inside a narrow storage closet beneath the gallery stairs.

46
00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:24.840
[George] He stood in pitch darkness, completely motionless, for two hours until the night watchman locked the heavy iron exterior doors, turned off the lights, and retired to his quarters with his guard dog.

47
00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:31.000
[George] At roughly two o'clock in the morning, Elser emerged into the echoing darkness of the empty beer hall.

48
00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:38.440
[George] Working by the dim beam of a pocket flashlight muffled with a handkerchief, Elser began chiseling into the solid stone pillar.

49
00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:40.840
[George] Every sound was a mortal danger.

50
00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:44.760
[George] Every blow of his hammer echoed off the vaulted wooden ceilings.

51
00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:53.560
[George] Whenever a streetcar rumbled past on the street outside, or when the night watchman stirred, Elser timed his hammer strikes to the ambient noise.

52
00:06:53.560 --> 00:07:03.400
[George] Using hand chisels, Elser hollowed out a cavity two and a half feet high, one foot wide, and one foot deep directly into the structural heart of the pillar.

53
00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:11.560
[George] Every ounce of stone dust, rubble and plaster had to be collected meticulously in burlap sacks and carried up into the gallery.

54
00:07:11.560 --> 00:07:22.080
[George] Before dawn, Elser swept the floor with a tiny brush, washed away the chalk dust with a damp rag, and hid in the closet until the doors unlocked at seven in the morning.

55
00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:30.320
[George] He would then slip out onto the Munich streets, his coat pockets weighted with heavy stone fragments, which he scattered into the Isar River.

56
00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:38.400
[George] He repeated this agonizing, terrifying ritual for thirty consecutive nights over two months, losing twenty pounds from exhaustion.

57
00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:42.720
[George] By late October, the cavity in the stone pillar was complete.

58
00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:56.560
[George] To conceal the hollow, Elser crafted an exquisite false wooden paneling that matched the surrounding oak wainscoting down to the finest grain, secured with hidden brass springlatches that could only be opened with a secret magnetic pin.

59
00:07:56.560 --> 00:08:07.200
[George] Inside the cavity, Elser lined the stone walls with heavy sheet metal and thick layers of dark green velvet cork to prevent the ticking of his clockwork from echoing into the hall.

60
00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:11.440
[George] Now came the true mechanical miracle, the timing mechanism.

61
00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:19.520
[George] Elser knew that standard alarm clocks could only run for twelve hours, which was far too short to allow him to escape across the border.

62
00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:26.080
[George] Using two high-grade eight-day Swiss clock movements, Elser constructed an ingenious dual movement timer.

63
00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:28.240
[George] The two clocks ran in parallel.

64
00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:34.640
[George] If one clock stopped or suffered a broken mainspring, the second clock would still trip the firing lever.

65
00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:41.040
[George] The gears were calibrated to tick down for 144 consecutive hours, six full days.

66
00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:54.880
[George] At the zero hour, a tripwire would release a heavy steel lever, driving a firing pin into a live rifle cartridge, which in turn detonated the fulminate of mercury caps, igniting the hundred-pound dynamite charge.

67
00:08:54.880 --> 00:09:01.800
[George] On the night of November 5, Elser installed the explosive charge and the clockwork mechanism inside the pillar.

68
00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:10.360
[George] On the night of November 6, he returned to the hall, pressed his ear against the wood, and listened to the steady, muffled heartbeat of his creation.

69
00:09:10.360 --> 00:09:13.800
[George] On November 7, he set the final timer.

70
00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:17.480
[George] The bomb was scheduled to detonate at precisely 9.20 p.m.

71
00:09:17.480 --> 00:09:19.240
[George] on the evening of November 8.

72
00:09:19.240 --> 00:09:24.280
[George] Hitler was scheduled to begin speaking at 8.30 and conclude around 10 o'clock.

73
00:09:24.280 --> 00:09:28.040
[George] The blast would strike directly at the climax of his speech.

74
00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:34.680
[George] On the morning of November 8, 1939, Elser boarded a train heading toward the Swiss border.

75
00:09:34.680 --> 00:09:43.800
[George] Meanwhile in Munich, the Bujabreukler was packed to the rafters with 3,000 high-ranking Nazi officials, SS commanders and stormtroopers.

76
00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:51.000
[George] At 8 o'clock, Adolf Hitler arrived, flanked by Rudolf Hess, Joseph Goebbels and Heinrich Himmler.

77
00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:58.280
[George] The Fu Remounted the Speaker's podium, leaning against the wooden lectern positioned just six feet in front of the stone pillar.

78
00:09:58.280 --> 00:10:02.120
[George] The crowd erupted in thunderous roars of Siegheil.

79
00:10:02.120 --> 00:10:05.800
[George] Outside Munich, however, the weather had turned sour.

80
00:10:05.800 --> 00:10:13.400
[George] A thick, impenetrable autumn fog had rolled in across southern Germany, grounding all commercial and military aircraft.

81
00:10:13.400 --> 00:10:20.040
[George] Hitler had originally planned to fly back to Berlin that night to finalize war plans for the invasion of France.

82
00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:30.280
[George] Informed by his aides that flying was impossible, Hitler was forced to take the night sleeper train, which was scheduled to depart the Munich Central Station at 9.30.

83
00:10:30.280 --> 00:10:35.080
[George] Because of the train's schedule, Hitler uncharacteristically cut his speech short.

84
00:10:35.080 --> 00:10:40.520
[George] Instead of his usual two-hour tirade, Hitler concluded abruptly at 9:07 p.m.

85
00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:49.040
[George] He stepped down from the podium, saluted the crowd, and hurried out of the hall into his waiting Mercedes, speeding toward the train station.

86
00:10:44.680 --> 00:10:56.000
[George] Thirteen minutes later, at precisely 9.20 p.m., the empty podium stood silent under the chandeliers.

87
00:10:56.000 --> 00:11:00.080
[George] And then, the pillar detonated with the force of an artillery shell.

88
00:11:00.080 --> 00:11:03.920
[George] The explosion blew the stone column into pulverized powder.

89
00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:09.520
[George] A shockwave tore through the hall, shattering every window and blowing out the front doors.

90
00:11:09.520 --> 00:11:18.720
[George] Instantly, the wooden balcony and the massive structural ceiling collapsed into the center of the room in an avalanche of plaster, heavy timber, and concrete.

91
00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:25.600
[George] The speaker's podium where Hitler had been standing thirteen minutes earlier was buried beneath eight feet of crushed rubble.

92
00:11:25.600 --> 00:11:32.880
[George] Eight people were killed instantly, including seven veteran Nazi officials, and sixty-three were seriously injured.

93
00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:40.480
[George] If Hitler had spoken for just 14 minutes longer, the entire upper tier of the Nazi regime would have been exterminated.

94
00:11:40.480 --> 00:11:48.400
[George] Thirty minutes before the explosion, 36-year-old Georg Elser was stopped at the border crossing in Konstanz, Switzerland.

95
00:11:48.400 --> 00:11:54.160
[George] A routine border guard noticed that Elser had an expired passport and searched his pockets.

96
00:11:54.160 --> 00:12:03.120
[George] Inside his coat, the guards found wire cutters, detonator diagrams, and a color postcard of the interior of the Munich Bujabriukular.

97
00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:12.160
[George] When news of the beer hall explosion flashed across police teletypes an hour later, Elser was immediately transferred into the custody of the Gestapo.

98
00:12:12.160 --> 00:12:20.080
[George] For days, Gestapo chief Heinrich Muller and Heinrich Himmler personally interrogated and tortured Elser with medieval brutality.

99
00:12:20.080 --> 00:12:28.240
[George] Hitler and the Nazi leadership refused to believe that a solitary German worker could have conceived and executed such a sophisticated plot.

100
00:12:28.240 --> 00:12:35.400
[George] They were convinced that Elser was a puppet of British intelligence, or a tool of Otto Strasser's anti-Hitler faction.

101
00:12:35.400 --> 00:12:40.760
[George] Yet, under relentless physical agony, Elser repeated the exact same truth.

102
00:12:40.760 --> 00:12:51.880
[George] He had acted completely alone, without accomplices, without outside money, motivated solely by his Christian conscience and his desire to save humanity from the horrors of total war.

103
00:12:51.880 --> 00:13:05.480
[George] To prove his confession, the Gestapo forced Elser to recreate his clockwork mechanism in a workshop from memory, and watching his astonishing mechanical mastery, his captors realized to their shock that he was telling the truth.

104
00:13:05.480 --> 00:13:15.160
[George] Hitler ordered Elser kept alive as a special prisoner at Dachau concentration camp, intending to stage a grand show trial after Germany won the war.

105
00:13:15.160 --> 00:13:24.680
[George] On April 9, 1945, just weeks before the fall of Berlin and the liberation of the camps, Hitler issued the personal execution order.

106
00:13:31.960 --> 00:13:38.520
[George] His body was immediately cremated to ensure that no grave could ever become a shrine to his memory.

107
00:13:56.200 --> 00:14:06.680
[Rebecca] His story reminds us that history is shaped not only by dictators and armies, but by the solitary convictions of everyday individuals who refuse to look away from evil.

108
00:14:06.680 --> 00:14:09.720
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

109
00:14:09.720 --> 00:14:11.000
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

110
00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:14.920
[Rebecca] Join us next time as we unearth another forgotten chapter from the pages of history.

111
00:14:15.680 --> 00:14:18.160
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

